# Mutation cases for §scxml-3.13 read against the case the specification
# explicitly allows — a macrostep whose eventless chain never ends.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/eventless_macrostep_is_bounded.cases`.
#
# The clause defines a macrostep as a chain of microsteps ending in a
# configuration where nothing is enabled by NULL. The specification's
# Principles and Constraints then say that chain need not exist: "A microstep
# always terminates. A macrostep may not. ... This is currently allowed." So a
# cyclic eventless document is conformant and an engine that runs it to the
# letter never returns — measured 2026-08-20, the Python engine did exactly
# that, while five others stopped the chain and said nothing a program could
# read and the C++ AOT engine stopped the machine.
#
# The trap these cases exist for is the second one: a ceiling that fires on any
# long chain reports every document that computes before it settles as a
# runaway. The fixture's bounded chain is exactly the ceiling's length and must
# pass through untouched, and `laps` / `spins` are what say which of the two
# the engine did.
#
# The C++ engine halves and the C11 template live in
# `eventless_macrostep_is_bounded_ctest.cases`.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-rust-tests --test eventless_macrostep_is_bounded
mutation_targets backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs
mutation_oracles backends/rust/tests/tests/eventless_macrostep_is_bounded.rs

# ── The refusal itself ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The engine keeps walking the chain. This is the mutation that used to be the
# shipped behaviour on the one engine with no ceiling at all, so the case is
# also the regression: with no budget the drain does not come back, and the
# harness's artefact timeout is what turns "the round hung" into a verdict.

mutation_case "the macrostep past the ceiling is taken anyway" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """                sce_log_error!(
                    "Engine::check_eventless_transitions: macrostep still going after {} microsteps; stopped",
                    MAX_MACROSTEP_MICROSTEPS
                );
                break;""",
     """                sce_log_error!(
                    "Engine::check_eventless_transitions: macrostep still going after {} microsteps; stopped",
                    MAX_MACROSTEP_MICROSTEPS
                );""")
PY

# ── The chain that ends on its own ─────────────────────────────────
#
# Counting loop turns rather than microsteps taken spends the budget on the
# turn that finds nothing enabled, so a chain that settles exactly at the
# ceiling is reported as a runaway. That is not hypothetical: both C++ engines
# shipped this off-by-one, and in the AOT one the verdict called `stop()`.
# `a_chain_that_ends_at_the_ceiling_is_not_refused` is the assertion that
# separates them, and it is why the fixture's bounded chain is exactly the
# ceiling's length rather than three.

mutation_case "a chain that settles at the ceiling is called a runaway" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """            let took_transition = self.process_transition_dispatch(&mut new_state, null_event);
            if !took_transition {
                // Nothing is enabled by NULL — the macrostep has
                // reached the stable configuration the clause describes, and
                // nothing was refused however long the chain was.
                break;
            }

            if self.macrostep_microsteps_taken == MAX_MACROSTEP_MICROSTEPS {""",
     """            let took_transition = self.process_transition_dispatch(&mut new_state, null_event);
            if self.macrostep_microsteps_taken == MAX_MACROSTEP_MICROSTEPS {""")
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """            self.macrostep_microsteps_taken += 1;

            self.current_state = new_state;""",
     """            self.macrostep_microsteps_taken += 1;
            if !took_transition {
                break;
            }

            self.current_state = new_state;""")
PY

# ── The budget is per macrostep, not per caller ────────────────────
#
# The drain is reached twice for one external event — once from
# `execute_transition` and once from §scxml-D-mainEventLoop's own loop. Without
# the flag each caller gets a fresh budget, so the machine takes twice the
# microsteps it was allowed and reports two refusals for one macrostep. This is
# the defect the first run of the Rust channel found, and `spins == 500` with
# `truncated_macrosteps() == 1` is the pair that holds it.

mutation_case "each caller of the drain gets its own budget" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """        if self.macrostep_truncated {
            // This macrostep was already stopped at the ceiling. Re-entering
            // the drain would hand the same chain a second budget, which is
            // the runaway the ceiling exists to refuse.
            return;
        }""",
     """        // MUTANT: every caller starts the budget again.""")
PY

# ── The budget is per macrostep, not per machine ───────────────────
#
# The opposite error: a flag that is never cleared makes the ceiling a property
# of the machine's whole life. The second macrostep is refused before it runs,
# so a host polling the count sees it stop moving and reads the machine as
# recovered. `a_second_truncated_macrostep_counts_again` reads
# `spins == 1000`, which is the only assertion that can tell "bounded twice"
# from "bounded once, then inert".

mutation_case "the ceiling outlives the macrostep that hit it" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """        self.macrostep_truncated = false;
        self.macrostep_microsteps_taken = 0;
        {
            // §scxml-6.5: Execute finalize before parent's own transition matching""",
     """        {
            // §scxml-6.5: Execute finalize before parent's own transition matching""")
PY

# ── The count ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The chain is still cut, so nothing hangs and every configuration assertion
# still passes — the machine simply stops saying that the configuration it is
# handing back is not a stable one, which is the whole finding. This is the
# case that fails if a driver checks only that the call came back.

mutation_case "the refusal is silent" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """        self.truncated_macrosteps = self.truncated_macrosteps.saturating_add(1);""",
     """        self.truncated_macrosteps = self.truncated_macrosteps.saturating_add(0);""")
PY

# ── Naming where it stopped ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A count says a document somewhere cannot settle; the state says which walk.
# Naming the wrong end of the refused transition is invisible to every
# assertion except the one that asks: the fixture's cycle alternates two
# states, so the source and the target it would have entered are different
# names for the same refusal, and only one of them is where an author looks.
# (Dropping the name entirely is the sibling mutant in
# `internal_chain_is_bounded.cases`, which owns the shared recorder.)

mutation_case "the engine names the state the refused microstep would have entered" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """                self.record_truncated_macrostep(old_state);""",
     """                self.record_truncated_macrostep(new_state);""")
PY

# ── The lower bound on the accessor ────────────────────────────────
#
# An accessor that always names a state reads exactly like a working one from
# the assertion side, because the state enum's zero value is a real state.
# `an_ordinary_macrostep_is_not_counted` and the `None` check in the bounded
# case are the two that refuse it.

mutation_case "the machine always names a state it stopped in" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """    pub fn last_truncated_macrostep_state(&self) -> Option<P::State> {
        self.last_truncated_macrostep_state
    }""",
     """    pub fn last_truncated_macrostep_state(&self) -> Option<P::State> {
        Some(self.last_truncated_macrostep_state.unwrap_or(self.current_state))
    }""")
PY
